## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of nanomaterials, the related research fields, such as nanodrug and nanotoxicity, are also developing rapidly. Metal–organic framework (MOFs) materials have been widely used in the industries, including catalysis [1–3], separation [4–6], and gas storage [7]) because of their excellent structural characteristics, such as adjustable pore channels and large specific surface area. By contrast, the application of MOFs in medicine lags far behind. In 2014, numerous nanoscale MOFs (NMOFs) with novel structure and good biocompatibility were published; afterward, the potential clinical drug use of NMOFs was regarded by researchers and gradually became a topic of interest in MOFs materials [8,9]. The special physical and chemical properties of NMOFs make them innovative in the fields of specific targeting and treatment. Current research in this field mainly focuses on the use of adjustable channels and biocompatibility as delivery systems, such as drug, protein, nucleic acid, and polypeptide [10–12], and the application of the physical and chemical properties of materials as diagnostic and therapeutic drugs, such as photothermal therapy [13], magnetic resonance contrast agent, and fluorescent probe [14]. Although the small size and adjustable characteristics of MOFs materials have considerable innovative potential for the future development of medicines, many questions on their behavior in the physiological system still remain in the proof-of-concept stage.
