When nanoparticles (NPs) enter the physiological system, some biological macromolecules attach to their surfaces in a thermodynamically favorable manner, thereby forming a protein corona, which was first proposed by Cedervall et al. [15] in 2007 and can be divided into hard and soft coronas. This process is a dynamic equilibrium process intensely related to surface properties and proteins. The state of reaching quasi-steady state is defined as protein corona fingerprint [16]. After NPs interact with the physiological system, the protein corona formed on the NP surface will provide it with new biorecognition properties because the binding of specific regions to NP surface will change its colloidal stability, toxicity, and cell absorption [17]. Protein corona formation may also induce the latent effect of NPs on macrophages, which may be positive or negative effects. Therefore, determining the biocompatibility only via bare NPs is insufficient. Meanwhile, the interaction between protein and NPs will also affect the activity, secondary structure, and other important properties of the protein itself. Protein transformation may also expose or encapsulate some specific active sites, thereby affecting its physiological function [18]. Kong et al. [19] studied the cell autophagy responses between protein corona and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NPs with different sizes, morphologies, and compositions. Results showed that the protein corona can change the cell autophagy level to some extent. Chaudhary et al. [20] investigated the effect of NPs with different shapes on the secondary structure of human serum albumin (HSA), which
indicated that Au NPs with different morphologies can induce protein corona with different structures and compositions. Chen et al. [16] investigated the uptake and transfection of different PEG-bound nanoliposomes by cells in the presence or absence of albumin. Albumin plays a key role in this cell interaction. Similarly, Su et al. [21] found that the formation of protein corona significantly changed the active and passive targeting functions of Au polyethylene glycol NPs that are modified by different cyclic RGD peptides. The results of numerous studies showed that the interaction between NPs and biological macromolecules cannot be ignored when NPs enter the blood system. However, to our knowledge, although many studies are devoted to the study of NMOFs-based drug delivery system, few reports have focused on the protein corona formed on NMOFs. In view of the unique physical and chemical properties of NMOFs, this special corona phenomenon may be relatively different from that in other NP systems.
Uio series MOFs are constructed from different organic ligands and metal ion Zr with \( \mathrm{Zr}_{6} \mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4} \) octahedral as the secondary building units and 12 linear dicarboxylic acid organic ligands connected in three dimensions to form porous networks. Uio series MOFs have been widely used in gas adsorption, organic dye adsorption, fluorescence sensing, drug-sustained release, and photocatalysis due to their advantages, such as good chemical stability, adjustable morphology, high thermal stability, and large specific surface area [22]. Many researchers are working on the synthesis of new materials on the basis of Uio series MOFs. For instance, Wang et al. [12] obtained Uio66 with adjustable size and pore size by monoacid MA and TEA and regressed its surface-modified mesoporous dioxide to realize membrane impermeable protein loading. Schrimpf et al. [23] revealed defects in the Uio67 crystal structure by fluorescence lifetime imaging, thereby providing new insight into MOFs materials.
To investigate the role of morphology in protein adsorption on the NMOFs surface, we selected Uio66 and Uio67 that have been applied in many research fields mentioned above as NP models. There are slight differences in organic ligands (Uio67 contains one more benzene ring which does not react with amino acids in proteins under experimental conditions) between them, resulting in significant differences in morphology (Fig. 1) and slight difference in pore size (Figs. S6–7). The surface parameters of protein such as roughness, charge distribution or steric areas points seriously affect the adsorption onto nanoparticles, as well the flexibility of the protein is also play a key role in this process [24]. The model protein selected in this study was HSA, which is a target or model protein that is widely used in the study of protein–ligand interaction. At the same time, HSA has attracted wide attention from
pharmaceutical researchers because of its good biocompatibility and nontoxic properties [25,26].
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All chemical reagents were obtained commercially from analytical grade and without further purification. Fatty-acid-free human serum albumin (A1887-5G, HSA) was purchased from Sigma Aldrich (St. Louis, MO, USA). Anhydrous zirconium (IV) chloride (99.95%), p-Phthalic acid (BDC, 98%), biphenyl-4,4-dicarboxylic acid (BPDC, 98%), calcein (98%) and \( p \)-nitrophenyl acetate (p-NPA, 98%) were obtained from J&K Scientific Ltd. (Beijing, China). Anhydrous ethanol, methanol, dimethylformamide (DMF) and trimethylamine (TEA), concentrated hydrochloric acid (HCl) were purchased from Ke-Long Chemical Reagent Factory (Chengdu, China). Phosphate buffered solution dry powder (0.01 M, PBS, pH 7.2–7.4) was obtained from Solarbio Scientific Ltd. (Beijing, China). Dulbecco's modification of Eagle's medium (DMEM) and fetal bovine serum (FBS) were purchased from ScienceCell Research Laboratories (USA). Hoechst 33,258 (trichochloride) was purchased from MedChenExpress (USA). Milli-Q water was used throughout the experiment.
## 2.2. Uio66 and Uio67 synthesis
Uio66 and Uio67 were synthesized via the solvothermal synthesis method, following the method of Lizard et al. [27]. Briefly, for Uio66, 209 mg ZrCl₄ was dissolved in 12 mL of DMF, and 149 mg BDC was dissolved in 13 mL of DMF with another vial. Both solutions were completely dissolved ultrasonically and mixed together. Afterward, 1.5 mL of acetic acid and 37.5 μL of HCl were added.
For Uio67, 80 mg ZrCl₄ was dissolved in 12 mL of DMF, and 83 mg BPDC was dissolved in 13 mL of DMF with another vial. Both solutions were completely dissolved ultrasonically and mixed together. Then, 1.5 mL of acetic acid and 1180 μL of TEA (0.71 mol/L, diluted in DMF) were added. The mixed solutions were reacted in an oven at 120 °C for 24 h to yield white precipitates, which were collected by centrifugation (4600 rpm, 10 min), washed with DMF (×1) and methanol (×3), and dried overnight in a vacuum oven at 60 °C.
## 2.3. HSA adsorption in Uio66 and Uio67
A total of 120 mg Uio66 or Uio67 were suspended in 5 mL of 10 mg/mL HSA solution in PBS buffer solution, which was similar to the protein concentration in the commonly used cell culture environment. The mixed solutions were gently stirred at 25 °C for 2 h. The precipitates were centrifuged (8000 rpm, 10 min), washed with PBS once, and dried overnight under vacuum oven at 37 °C.
## 2.4. MTT assay
2.4. MTT assayThe cytotoxicity of Uio–calcein and calcein (control) were analyzed through a standard MTT assay by using HeLa cells (cervical cancer cells). Approximately 5000 cells were inoculated in a 96-well plate. After incubation in cell culture environment for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), the drug with increased concentration gradient (0, 5, 10, 20, 40, \(80\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\)) was added. After \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) of incubation, \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of MTT (PBS, \(5\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\)) was added. The cells were further incubated for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\). Then, the medium was carefully aspirated, and \(200\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of DMSO was added to each well to dissolve the purple crystals. Absorbance at \(570\,\mathrm{nm}\) was measured using a microplate reader (Molecular Devices, USA).
## 2.5. CLSM measurements
2.5. CLSM measurementsLive cell imaging was performed using Leica TCS SP5 II system. Briefly, approximately \(2\times10^{4}\). Hela cells were seeded into round coverslip placed in 12-well tissue culture plates (Corning, NY), followed by adding \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) Uio66-calcin, Uio67-calcein, or calcein dispersed in DMEM with the presence of \(0\%\), \(10\%\), and \(20\%\) FBS, respectively. After \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) of incubation, the media were carefully aspirated, and cells were washed with DPBS for two times. Then, the cells were stained by \(500\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of Hoechst 33258 (PBS, \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\)). After \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) of staining in cell culture environment, the media were changed to fresh DMEM before imaging. Hoechst emission and calcein were recorded at 460 (blue) and \(530\,\mathrm{nm}\) (green), respectively. All pictures were processed via LAS X software from Leica.
## 2.6. Flow cytometry measurements
2.6. Flow cytometry measurementsTo quantify the cellular uptake of Uio66 and Uio67 loaded with the fluorescent probe calcein, we seeded approximately \(4\times10^{5}\) Hela cells into 8-well tissue culture plates in DMEM with the presence of \(0\%\), \(10\%\), and \(20\%\) FBS, respectively. After \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) of incubation, \(120\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(1\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) NP stock solution (PBS, Uio66-calcin, Uio67-calcein, or calcein) was added to a final concentration of \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\). After another \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) of incubation, the media were carefully aspirated, and the cells were washed with DPBS two times. Then, the cells were harvested by trypsin digestion and centrifuged. Finally, the collected cells were resuspended in DPBS solution for flow cytometric analysis. Data were obtained from FACSCalibur Cytometer, FACSMelody (Becton Dickinson, USA).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Uio characterization
3. Results and discussion3.1. Uio characterizationUio66 and Uio67 NP syntheses were guided by the study of Lzaro et al. [27] with a minimal regulation. The PXRD spectra of the synthesized MOFs (Fig. 2) showed clear Bragg peaks (Uio66: \(7.36^{\circ}\), \(8.48^{\circ}\), \(12.04^{\circ}\); Uio67: \(5.6^{\circ}\), \(6.5^{\circ}\), \(11.4^{\circ}\)) within the measurement range, thereby indicating the formation of the expected MOFs structures. Uio66 (Fig. 2A) and Uio67 (Fig. 2B) NMOFs were presented as nearly circular and octahedral, respectively. The average sizes of Uio66 and Uio67 were estimated by SEM to be approximately 205.29 (Fig. S1A) and \(202.24\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S1B), respectively. The homogeneous distribution of the chemical elements of samples was also confirmed by energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping (Fig. S2). The samples were dispersed into PBS to characterize the dispersion and hydrodynamic diameters of the particles (Fig. S3). The average sizes of Uio66 and Uio67 were 257.5 and \(255.5\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. The PDI values were 0.178 and 0.232, which were lower than 0.3, thereby indicating that the prepared NPs had a narrow size distribution in the solution and good dispersion. The size of samples in PBS increased slightly because of the water molecules absorbed onto its surface, thereby resulting in a large dynamic diameter and a highly flexible structure [28].
## 3.2. Uio–HSA conjugate interaction
3.2. Uio–HSA conjugate interactionTo simulate protein crown formation in physiological system, we dispersed the samples in \(10\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) HSA SEM images showed that the morphology of all samples became round due to the adsorption of a layer of protein, and the average particle sizes of Uio66 and Uio67 increased to 325.45 and \(323.40\,\mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Fig. S4). The DLS profiles of Uio–HSA conjugates (Fig. S5) indicated that the peak value increased significantly, which indicated HSA adsorption on the sample surface. In particular, the aggregation phenomenon appeared in the Uio67–HSA system; this phenomenon will be further analyzed in the following section. Conjugate powder was characterized by PXRD to prove that Uio retained its crystallinity after adsorbing HSA, but its strength was lower than those of bare Uio66 and Uio67 (Fig. 3A). This result indicated that HSA adsorption resulted in crystal defects, while the overall skeleton remained unchanged.
Bare samples showed negative potential (Uio66: \(-17\;\mathrm{mV}\), Uio67: \(-18.5\;\mathrm{mV}\)) in PBS \((\mathrm{pH}=7.4)\) solution because of the dephosphorization of surface carboxylic acid. HSA was negative in physiological environment \((-10.2\;\mathrm{mV}\), Fig. 3B) [29]. When Uio66 and Uio67 with negative potential adsorbed HSA, the potential conjugates were smaller than those of bare samples (Uio66–HSA: \(-12.2\;\mathrm{mV}\), Uio67–HSA: \(-12.3\;\mathrm{mV}\)). Although albumin is negatively charged as a whole, it contains \(>60\) positive amino acids, such as Lys, Arg, and His, which interact with carboxylic acid groups on the surface of Uio samples; this phenomenon also indirectly proves that a strong electrostatic force occurred between them [30], but due to the complexity of the system, we can not exclude the existence of other forces. The high specific surface area and porosity of MOFs are the critical properties of their applications. BET characterization (Fig. 3C) showed that bare Uio samples and Uio–HSA conjugates exhibited similar IV-type adsorption isotherms. The high adsorption of \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) at low \( \mathrm{p}/\mathrm{p}^{\circ} \) indicated the presence of micropores [31]. The pore volume and size distribution of all samples were obtained by the Horvath–Kawazoe method, and the micropore structure of all systems was confirmed (Figs. S6–7). The \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}} \) of Uio67 decreased from \(746.8480\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) to \(11.2691\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), which indicated that the Uio67 surface was remarkably coated with proteins, which may have resulted in Uio67 NP aggregation. HSA considerably increased the \( \mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}} \) of Uio66, which may be related to the smaller pore size in Uio66–HSA conjugate than that in bare Uio66 (Fig. S6B). Fig. 3D shows the FTIR spectra of the samples. The IR spectra of HSA showed two characteristic bands, namely, amide I \((1600{-}1700\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(C{=}0\) tensile vibration) and amide II \((1500{-}1600\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(C{-}N\) tensile and \(N{-}H\) bending) which are closely related to the secondary HSA structure [32]. After combining with HSA, the peak intensity and position at \(1642\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) or \(1522\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of both samples shifted with varying degrees, and their fingerprint maps (dotted frame) changed significantly. The peak at
\(2361\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) also disappeared after Uio66 combined with HSA, and a new peak appeared at \(1305\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belonging to the stretching vibration absorption of C—N and C—O groups after Uio67 was combined with HSA. The disappearance of the peak of Raman spectra (Fig. S8) also suggested a chemical interaction between HSA and Uio samples. The characterization results showed that HSA interacted with Uio66 and Uio67, and the binding modes of the two systems may be different.
## 3.3. Study of protein corona
When NPs enter the physiological environment, they will actively interact with other molecules to reduce surface free energy and stabilize conformation [33]. The absorbed proteins can affect NP dispersion in the physiological atmosphere by improving the colloidal stability of the particles. Weiss et al. [34] divided the formation of protein corona into three phases. In the first phase, proteins bind reversibly on the particle surface; in the second phase, other proteins interact reversibly with previously absorbed proteins; in the last phase, soft corona binds reversibly. In both systems, TEM images (Fig. 4A) showed corona phenomenon. The heavy thick layer may be the soft corona because the unabsorbed proteins were washed away, whereas the soft corona part cannot be removed completely after washing up only once by centrifugation [35]. The element distribution of HSA on its surface was also visually observed through EDS mapping analysis (Fig. 4B), and the N content in Uio67 was \(1.11\%\) higher than that in Uio66 (Fig. S9). This result indicated that HSA was adsorbed substantially on the Uio67 surface. The TEM images of Uio66–HSA conjugate (Fig. 4A, left insert) showed the hard corona part of the protein corona, which was thin and close to the surface of Uio66.
For the Uio67–HSA system, we did not observe the hard corona part, but we observe particle aggregation, as previously mentioned earlier,
that is, five Uio67 NPs aggregated together (Fig. 4A, right). Wang et al. [36] found that the monolayer protein macromolecules formed on the polystyrene NP surface promoted their aggregation; however, as the number of protein adsorbed increased to saturation, the aggregation can be effectively inhibited after the particles reached colloidal stability. Under the same incubation conditions, Uio66 did not present the aggregation, thereby indicating that the adsorbed proteins on the Uio66 surface were nearly saturated. Nevertheless, the Uio67 surface still showed a single-layer protein, that is, it did not reach the colloidal stable state.
This phenomenon also proved that Uio67 may need to bind additional proteins to achieve colloidal stability status. Considering that protein concentration directly determines the amount of protein adsorbed on NP surface, we investigated the hydrodynamic diameter of bare Uio samples at different HSA concentrations. Fig. 4C shows that the peak value of DLS patterns of the Uio66 system increased with the increase in protein concentration, which can be ascribed to the fact that the number of protein adsorbed on its surface increased with the increase in protein concentration gradient. The results also showed that Uio66
exhibited a weak aggregation phenomenon. By contrast, with the increase in HSA concentration, the size of Uio67 particles and the aggregation phenomenon enhanced more significantly than those in the Uio66 system.
