Similarly, the mass of attached HSA may also be related to the binding constant [38]. The mass percentage of HSA that was bound to Uio66 and Uio67 surfaces was investigated by TGA characterization (Fig. 5B). HSA had a weight loss peak at approximately \(310\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which coincided with that of Uio66 but can be distinguished from that of Uio67. The mass percentages of HSA adsorbed on Uio66 and Uio67 surfaces were approximately \(4.14\%\) and \(9.61\%\), respectively. The size and morphology
of NP affect its binding constants with proteins [39]. The octahedron structure of Uio67 may make it binding with HSA stronger, which further increased the protein-binding amount on the surface to achieve its quasi-steady state. The results were also consistent with EDS mapping. The final weight loss of the two systems was less than that of bare Uio samples after HSA was compounded, thereby indicating that the organic contents of the two systems were reduced instead in contrast to intuitive judgment. The possible explanation here was the fact that the special coating layer of the Uio samples was formed by the acetic acid used in the synthesis process before they dispersed in the HSA solution. Acetic acid was linked with the exposed Zr ion on NP surface through the carboxylic acid group; consequently, the carboxylic and amino acid groups of HSA competed with acetic acid for Zr ion sites, thereby resulting in the entropically favored ligand exchange [40]. These studies showed that Uio67 should be bound to additional HSA to form a saturated single-layer protein molecule and showed strong binding with HSA. The reason for this difference may be closely related to the surface morphologies of Uio67 and Uio66.
## 3.4. Effects on protein conformation
When protein is adsorbed on the NP surface, it changes its conformation to stabilize the molecular network in the new physicochemical environment [41]. The conformational changes of proteins can be explored directly by quenching the endogenous fluorescence (especially Tyr). Fig. 6A shows the 3D fluorescence spectra of three systems. Peaks A and B represent Rayleigh \(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}\) and second-order scattering peaks \(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=2\;\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}\), respectively. Peak 1 is the fluorescence characteristic peak of Trp and Tyr residues. Peak 2 is related to the helix and curl
structure of HSA [42]. The graph showed that the intensity of all characteristic peaks decreased in varying degrees after HSA was adsorbed on the Uio sample surface. The decrease in the intensity of peak A suggested that the interaction between Uio samples and HSA can increase the molecular diameter of HSA. Uio67 had a greater impact on the Trp and Tyr residues of HSA with the significant decrease in peak 1 intensity in the Uio67–HSA system. The study of specific Trp and Tyr residue microenvironments in synchronous fluorescence spectroscopy (Fig. S12) showed the same trend as that in 3D spectroscopy. Fig. 6A illustrates that Uio66 or Uio67 had a similar effect on peak 2, which represented the secondary structure information of HSA. We quantitatively detected the \(\upbeta\) -helix content of the secondary HSA structure by CD
spectroscopy (Fig. 6B). The results showed that Uio66 and Uio67 increased the \(\upbeta\) -helix proportion from \(53.119\%\) to \(57.500\%\) and \(58.281\%\) respectively. This slight increase was consistent with the 3D spectra results. Generally, the change in the characteristic peaks in the Uio67–HSA system was stronger than that in the Uio66–HSA system, especially for peaks A and 1. The reason for this phenomenon may be related to the small surface curvature of Uio67. The protein conformation on the surface of high-curvature sphere tended to remain unchanged. Meanwhile, on the surface of low-curvature octahedron, conformation changes occurred to stabilize conformation.
HSA is an important carrier functional protein in human blood. The conformational transformation of HSA may affect its transport of
endogenous molecules, such as fatty acids, cholesterol, steroids, and other exogenous therapeutic drugs [18]. The study of the esterase-like activity of HSA has been a topic of interest in the field of protein–ligand interaction because it plays an important role in the normal operation of physiological functions in human blood. The key parameters were calculated using the double-derivative Lineweaver–Burk plot of Michaelis–Menten equation (Fig. S13). The results are summarized in Table S2. For the Uio66–HSA system, the affinity of HSA to substrate PNPA increased as the Michaelis constant \( \mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}} \) decreased (Fig. 6C). \( \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{max}} \) value decreased, and the \( \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{max}}/\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}} \) ratio remained unchanged, thereby suggesting that Uio66 only bound to enzyme–substrate complex but not to free enzyme. This result proved that Uio66 inhibited the esterase-like activity of HSA in an anticompetitive manner. For the Uio67–HSA system, the affinity between HSA and P-NPA decreased with the increase in \( \mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}} \) value, and the \( \mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{max}} \) value was almost unchanged, which implied that Uio67 can compete with the P-NPA for the activity center of HSA. This condition would inhibit the binding of the enzyme to the substrate to form intermediate products.
HSA presented conformation turbulence in varying degrees after binding with Uio66 and Uio67. This turbulence changed the exposure or embedding of some active sites in HSA molecule, thereby affecting the esterase-like activity of HSA. The change in protein conformation mainly came from the direct binding part of NPs, namely, hard corona. Thus, the morphology of NPs directly determines protein conformation. This study also showed that Uio67 with regular octahedron shape made the HSA conformation more disturbed than circular Uio66 did.
## 3.5. Cellar interaction
The interaction of NPs with cells and organisms is regulated by the biological macromolecules adsorbed on their surfaces, that is, protein corona. To investigate the effect of protein corona on the cellular uptake of Uio66 and Uio67 as drug transmembrane carriers, we dispersed bare samples in the DMEM medium containing \( 0\% \), \( 10\% \), and \( 20\% \) FBS to form protein corona with different thicknesses. Calcein was used as fluorescent probe postsynthetically loaded into Uio66 and Uio67
micropores to detect intracellular differentiation. Calcein's hydrophilicity \(\left(\log P=-4\right)\) makes it unable to penetrate the cell membrane effectively; hence, it is widely used as a fluorescent probe for cellular uptake [43]. As shown in Fig.s S14–15, calcein was successfully loaded into micropores and did not change the structures of the samples.
In view of our system, we first tested the cytotoxicity of samples. MTT assay showed that the NPs studied had insignificant inhibitory effect on HeLa cells (Fig. S16), thereby suggesting further intracellular experiments can be carried out. Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) presented that that only an extremely small amount of green fluorescence (calcein) appeared in HeLa cells in incubation-native calcein (Fig. S17). This result agreed with the membrane impermeability of calcein. For both systems, the NP distribution in cells depended largely on the thickness of protein adsorbed on their surfaces (Fig. S18). Fig. 7A shows that when the FBS concentration was \(0\%\) , a small amount of green fluorescence appeared around the nucleus of HeLa cells, and most NPs gathered around the cell membrane. When incubated in \(10\%\) FBS DMEM, the green fluorescence around the nucleus increased significantly. However, when the FBS concentration increased to \(20\%\) , the green fluorescence around the nucleus seemed to decrease instead. In addition, in the Uio66 system, the intracellular uptake rate of NPs in all FBS concentrations was less than that of Uio67, but we thought that this phenomenon was caused by the higher loading rate of calcein for Uio67 (Uio66: \(3.40\%\) , Uio67: \(18.37\%\) , details in S1.4) than Uio66, and the effect of morphology needed further study.
To verify the CLSM results, we further applied flow cytometry to analyze the cellar uptake rate of Uio66 and Uio67 in different conditions quantitatively. Fig. 7B shows that the fluorescent intensity profiles of Uio66 and Uio67 shifted to the high-intensity region compared with the control group. As presented in Fig. 8, the percentage of internalized cells in both systems was consistent with the trend of CLSM results, ranging from \(1.58\%\) to \(21.5\%\) to \(17.0\%\) (Uio66) and \(14.9\%\) to \(69.9\%\) to \(66.8\%\) (Uio67) when incubated in \(0\%\) , \(10\%\) , and \(20\%\) FBS, respectively. Uio66 and Uio67 relied heavily on the formation of protein corona to enter cells, which is essential for most drug nanocarriers to play a role. Under \(10\%\) FBS, the intracellular rate was the highest, but the rate was not proportional to the FBS concentration. The results of CLSM and flow cytometry experiments showed the same trend and we speculate
that the thickness of absorbed protein is the critical factors determining the intracellular of NPs.
## 4. Conclusions
In summery, the current study carried out detailed adsorption studies in vitro and cell uptake tests for Uio66 and Uio67 and obtained promising preliminary results. The binding constants between Uio samples and HSA as well as the surface curvature determined the amount of binding protein to a certain extent. The octahedral Uio67 needed to bind with more HSA molecules to achieve colloidal stability state than the spherical Uio66. This phenomenon may be closely related to the fact that Uio67 required more HSA molecules to form monolayer protein macromolecules in view of the aggregate phenomenon in the Uio67– HSA system. Monolayer protein macromolecule, that is, hard corona, was directly related to the binding constant and the surface curvature of NPs. This hard corona part is easier to subject to conformation disturbance. Uio67 with octahedral shape tended to induce significant protein conformation change. The internalization of Uio66 and Uio67 by HeLa cells depended substantially on protein corona thickness. Both systems showed the same trend as the cellular uptake efficiency first increased and then decreased with the increase in protein concentration. However, the role of morphology in the process of intracellular has not been studied well here because of NMOFs modes selected lack of fluorescence characteristics.
Protein corona formation is a complicated process, and many mechanisms remain at a vague stage. Most studies at present in the field of NPs–protein are limited to in vitro environment. However, the process of protein corona formation in vitro is relatively different from that in real physiological environment. Weiss et al. [34] used microfluidic control and CLSM technology to realize in situ dynamic study of protein corona formation at the time–resolution level and proposed that the protein adsorption process can be divided into three stages. The effect of protein corona on those functional NMOFs (targeting, stability, etc.) after entering physiological environment is also an important factor that researchers should not ignore. For example, Oet al. [44] found that the protein corona made the targeted nanodrug lose its targeting capability and easy to be swallowed by macrophages, but the phenomenon was improved by precoating the recombinant fusion protein with NPs. Further work, including experiments in vitro with complex bioliquids (protein type, concentration, ionic strength, temperature, pH, etc.) and detailed in situ studies, should be performed to realize the clinical application of NMOF-based medicals and provide additional insight into protein corona.
